Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026
Notes: Period live-action drama spanning late-1970s childhood, 1998 Milwaukee, and eight years later Seattle.

FADE IN:

EXT. KIRK STREET ALLEY - DAY

Late 1970s. Children play kickball behind a brick apartment building, using dented garbage-can lids for bases.

MARK CONNER kicks the red rubber ball through MRS. ENGEL'S kitchen window. Glass crashes across her sink.

MRS. ENGEL appears at the broken window. Mark freezes, terrified.

Twelve-year-old NICK, already broad and strong, steps in front of the other children.

NICK
I did it.

LITTLE BROTHER (V.O.)
Big brother was mine. I'm not talking about that stupid book where everybody can see everything you're doing. Nick was my real Big Brother. He always stood up for me, no matter what.

Two decades later, the same alley. Nick's burnt-orange 1980 Pontiac Trans Am waits beneath the fire escape, yellow flames along its sides and a great eagle across its hood.

Adult NICK is a towering, heavily muscled man with unruly reddish-brown hair, scars on his chin and over his left eyebrow, a brown suede jacket, and heavy boots. He shoulders six grocery bags at once.

LITTLE BROTHER, his lean, straight-brown-haired sibling of about thirty, carries the last bag.

LITTLE BROTHER (V.O.)
People moved out of his way. His face looked like a puzzle held together with glue, and he had a pair on him the size of steel wrecking balls. To me, he was a modern-day Viking.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

A cramped fifth-floor apartment worn smooth by years of use.

MA, a slight woman in her mid-sixties with graying brown hair and tired brown eyes, sleeps beneath a gray afghan in her bedroom.

Nick stands in the doorway while Little Brother puts away groceries.

NICK
What's Ma doin' back in the bedroom at this time of day?

LITTLE BROTHER
I don't know. Why?

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. That's how old people go downhill. They start sleepin' all day, and pretty soon they don't want to go outside, or eat, or go to the store or nothing.

Little Brother lowers his eyes.

LITTLE BROTHER
Could you talk to your boss about getting me into the metal factory?

NICK
You don't need a degree to get a good job. All you need is to know somebody. All that college crap is just a bunch of nerds talking to hear themselves talk. The real world is here, where we live.

Nick passes him without answering the question.

LITTLE BROTHER (V.O.)
Nick was right. I was too old, and somebody had to take care of Ma. I did like reading, though. I didn't tell nobody that.

EXT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

Little Brother chains his bicycle outside the neighborhood pizza parlor. He wears a flour-dusted work shirt and jeans.

SIONNA, a slim woman in her late twenties with pale skin, shoulder-length brown hair, and striking light-blue eyes, trips over a crack in the pavement and falls against him. A silver fish pendant rests above her black V-neck shirt.

LITTLE BROTHER
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

She brushes off her shirt. They stare at each other, neither moving away.

LITTLE BROTHER
I work here. I'm just going to work. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

LITTLE BROTHER
Sionna? What does that mean?

SIONNA
An Irish goddess. The River Shannon.

LITTLE BROTHER
Do you want to come in and have some pizza?

SIONNA
No, thanks. I don't eat pizza. But if you want to have a cup of coffee sometime, you can call me.

She writes her number on a scrap of paper. As she walks away, she looks back over her shoulder.

LITTLE BROTHER (V.O.)
No girl had ever been interested in me. But the weird thing was, I knew her. It felt like the tide was coming in, and I didn't want to move my feet.

INT. MILWAUKEE PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

Old lamps hang on chains above long wooden tables. Towering shelves disappear into shadow.

Little Brother fills out a library-card form, then wanders among Shakespeare, Dickens, Dumas, and Wilde.

He pulls out WUTHERING HEIGHTS by Emily Brontë.

LITTLE BROTHER (V.O.)
It was like I could get anything in here for free. Who needed school when all of it was right here?

At a table, he begins reading. The bustle around him fades as he turns another page.

LITTLE BROTHER (V.O.)
It was about Heathcliff and Catherine. I got the feeling those two really loved each other. It reminded me of Sionna.

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

College students crowd a warm coffee shop with backpacks and textbooks.

Sionna sits opposite Little Brother. Her silver fish pendant catches the window light.

SIONNA
What are you reading right now?

LITTLE BROTHER
Wuthering Heights. It's the story of this guy and girl and how they grew apart but were really in love with each other.

SIONNA
The environment is like its people. Wild and unpredictable. Heathcliff's love for Catherine never changes, but in the end that kind of love destroys him.

She leans closer.

SIONNA
A love that never changes will destroy you.

Little Brother considers this, seeing Nick and Ma in a new light.

LITTLE BROTHER (V.O.)
Something inside of me was coming awake. She made me feel smarter just by being around her.

A community-college brochure rests near the register. Little Brother picks it up before they leave.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Some weeks later. A blue pill organizer labeled with the days of the week sits on the table beside blood-pressure medicine.

Ma studies it suspiciously while Little Brother loads the compartments.

MA
You're such a good boy. You and Nick are like a God-send to me.

Nick enters and picks up the organizer.

NICK
Oh, holy shit. Aren't you Nurse Nancy? How did you come up with this little idea?

LITTLE BROTHER
Sionna helped me. And I'm going to community college for a business degree.

NICK
This chick sure has you wrapped around her finger. How the hell are you going to pay for that? It won't be me helping your sorry ass when you finish, if you finish.

LITTLE BROTHER
It was my idea. It would mean a lot if you could be on my side for once.

NICK
Nothing comes between us. Not school, not Ma, and not this girl. Bro's before ho's. You get me?

LITTLE BROTHER
You're so Goddamn hateful. Was Ma on crack when she was pregnant with you?

Nick's open hand cracks across Little Brother's head.

NICK
Don't you ever talk that way about my mother.

Nick storms out. The door slams as Little Brother holds his ringing ear.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

Months later. Smoke, hard rock, beer bottles, and a crowded pool table.

Community-college books protrude from Little Brother's backpack. He takes a measured breath as Nick drinks beside him.

Nick spots his former girlfriend LINDSEY across the room with a dark-haired boyfriend.

NICK
Who's that asshole?

LITTLE BROTHER
Drop it, man. You broke up with her.

Nick crosses the room. Lindsey stiffens as her boyfriend places a protective arm around her.

LITTLE BROTHER
Nick, leave it alone. Lindsey can see whoever she wants.

Nick shoves Little Brother aside.

NICK
Did I ask your opinion?

LITTLE BROTHER steps directly into his brother's path.

LITTLE BROTHER
Stop it! No one cares about your ego, Nick!

NICK
Keep out of it. You're going to be next if you don't get out of my face.

Nick draws back his fist. Little Brother jerks away as the punch cuts past his cheek.

JOE and his broad-shouldered bouncer seize Nick. Nick head-butts the bouncer as the room erupts.

Little Brother bolts through the door and runs.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

Little Brother bursts inside, breathless. Books, a yoga mat, and handwritten class notes cover the living-room table.

Sionna sits him on the faded green sofa and touches his face.

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you.

LITTLE BROTHER
Yes, he did. I love Nick, but I don't know if we'll ever speak again.

SIONNA
No one does that who really cares about you. Look at the progress you've made. You have learned to use your brain. You can't let him intimidate you or stop you from your purpose.

LITTLE BROTHER
My purpose. What is my purpose?

SIONNA
To find the true self within you and honor it. To be the best person you can while you're here.

He rests his head in her lap.

LITTLE BROTHER
The first time I met you, it was like I had finally come home.

SIONNA
Me too, honey. Me too.

She kisses his forehead.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Sionna serves warm peach pie to Ma. Nick sits stiffly across from her, declining his plate.

NICK
I hear you're a university lady. Sometimes all that school just doesn't pay off. It's even harder for a woman to teach at that level.

SIONNA
There are more women in college today than men. Women bring different ways of thinking to the table. Diversity helps us learn.

Nick's face tightens. Ma beams at Sionna.

Weeks later, Nick carefully fills Ma's pill organizer and pockets his Trans Am keys.

NICK
I'm taking Ma to St. Patrick's for four o'clock mass.

Ma takes his arm. Little Brother watches them leave with relief mixed with unease.

LITTLE BROTHER (V.O.)
You don't know Nick like I do. You don't go from scary mother to escorting Ma to church. But I had been the caretaker for so long. I needed the break.

EXT. WERNER AND LOWE INTERSECTION - NIGHT

The burnt-orange Trans Am enters the intersection.

Nick drives with bloodshot eyes. Ma sits beside him in her church coat, a parish bulletin folded in her hands.

Headlights flare through the passenger window.

A sedan smashes broadside into the Trans Am.

Metal folds. Glass explodes. The Pontiac spins across the wet pavement and stops beneath a traffic light.

Nick hangs unconscious against his seat belt. Ma is utterly still.

INT. MILWAUKEE COUNTY SHERIFF'S STATION - DAY

OFFICER DUPUIS, a large man with graying hair and a thin mustache, sits opposite Little Brother.

LITTLE BROTHER
Where's Ma? Is she okay?

OFFICER DUPUIS
There has been a serious accident involving your brother, Nicholas Olson, and your mother. Your brother is in serious condition. Your mother passed away at the scene.

The rest of the room falls silent.

Little Brother tries to drink from a foam cup, but his hands shake too hard. Water spills across the desk.

LITTLE BROTHER
Can I call someone? Can I have a ride to the hospital?

OFFICER DUPUIS steadies the cup and slides the telephone toward him.

EXT. ST. MARY'S CEMETERY - DAY

A handful of mourners surround Ma's light maple casket.

Little Brother releases a fistful of earth. It strikes the lid with a muted THUNK.

Sionna takes his trembling hand.

LITTLE BROTHER (V.O.)
One day you're talking to her. The next day you're putting her in a box. The oldest son couldn't attend his own mother's funeral.

The casket descends beneath the trees.

LITTLE BROTHER (V.O.)
Ma was dead. And Nick killed her.

INT. FRODO HOSPITAL ICU - DAY

Five days after the crash. Nick lies bruised and yellow beneath white sheets. An oxygen tube runs into his nose, a chest tube drains his punctured lung, and his broken right leg is immobilized.

He switches off the television as Little Brother enters.

NICK
Hey, Bro. I guess I really fucked up. Can't believe Ma's gone.

Tears gather in Nick's bloodshot eyes.

LITTLE BROTHER
The mass was nice. She's at peace now.

NICK
I didn't mean to have three Jack and Cokes before I remembered I had to take her. Now the cops say vehicular manslaughter. It was a mistake. I was almost killed too.

LITTLE BROTHER
Whose fault was it, Nick?

NICK
You think I meant to kill her? You son of a bitch.

LITTLE BROTHER
Think about it, Nick.

Little Brother turns away.

LITTLE BROTHER (V.O.)
All his bullying and all his violence meant nothing at that moment. He couldn't hurt me now.

He leaves Nick trapped among the tubes and machines.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

Months later. Winter presses against the windows.

Nick, thinner and limping, stands over Sionna. Legal papers protrude from his brown suede jacket.

NICK
You're the reason I'm probably going to jail. If it wasn't for you messing with my brother, he would have been taking care of Ma. She would be alive right now.

SIONNA
Let's talk about this rationally. What makes you think I'm responsible?

NICK
You owe me for the hospital and the lawyers. Then you get away from my brother.

Sionna rises, but Nick blocks the hall. He clamps one hand around her neck and opens a folding knife beside her collarbone.

NICK
Break it off with him tonight. No more brother. No more contact. You got that?

The front door flies open. Little Brother sees the knife.

LITTLE BROTHER
Holy shit, Nick! What the fuck are you doing?

He grabs Nick's knife arm.

They struggle. Nick drives his elbow backward, and the blade plunges beneath Sionna's left collarbone.

Blood spreads across her shirt. Nick stares at what he has done, then bolts into the night.

Little Brother catches Sionna and lowers her to the floor. He presses both hands against the wound while dialing for help.

Sirens draw closer.

Paramedics lift Sionna onto a gurney. A monitor begins a steady BEEP as they carry her out.

LITTLE BROTHER (V.O.)
They told me later that if I hadn't walked in and pressed that wound, she would have died in the hallway.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - DAY

Months later. Sionna's shoulder remains stiff, and a scar rises above her camisole. The light has gone from her blue eyes.

She sits in a rocking chair while Little Brother waits on the sofa.

LITTLE BROTHER
Are you depressed? What are you thinking?

SIONNA
I can't feel me. I can't feel myself anymore.

He kneels before her.

LITTLE BROTHER
You are safe. I will protect you with my life.

SIONNA
You say that, but deep inside I still have my doubts. As long as there is any doubt, I can't go on with you. Maybe we need a break.

LITTLE BROTHER
See a therapist, but stay. Stay here with me. Please.

Sionna looks down at her hands, then walks into the bedroom and closes the door.

LITTLE BROTHER (V.O.)
The outer flesh healed. The inner wound lingered. She had awoken me from a monotonous life, but I couldn't make her stay.

Little Brother steps out of the duplex alone.

INT. TONY'S BOYS PIZZA OFFICE - DAY

Eight years later. Seattle rain streaks the office windows.

Little Brother is older and stronger, his brown hair touched with gray. A business degree hangs above a map marked with twelve TONY'S BOYS PIZZA locations.

He studies an old photograph of Ma standing behind two boys in homemade superhero capes.

A yoga mat lies beside his desk. He sits on it, closes his eyes, and breathes.

LITTLE BROTHER (V.O.)
Years of pain and growth had passed. Thanks to my old boss, Tony, I owned my own chain of pizzerias. But resentment kept me in my own prison.

He opens his eyes and looks at the photograph again.

LITTLE BROTHER (V.O.)
I finally understood that I had the key inside myself. I had to see Nick. I had to find forgiveness.

He opens his laptop and books a flight to Milwaukee.

INT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL INSTITUTION VISITING ROOM - DAY

Nick enters in prison clothes. His muscular frame has withered, his reddish-brown hair now thin and gray. Deep lines surround his watery eyes.

He sits behind thick plexiglass. Both brothers lift their yellow telephones.

NICK
Hi, Bro. Glad you came.

LITTLE BROTHER
We don't have much time, so I need you to listen.

Nick lowers his eyes.

LITTLE BROTHER
You killed my mother. You held me down my entire life so you could have the money and the exciting life. And you tried to kill the love of my life. Frankly, Nick, you're an asshole.

Nick absorbs every word.

LITTLE BROTHER
But this is the important bit. I forgive you. I forgive you because it sets me free from anger, regret, and loss. It releases your control over me forever.

Tears roll down Nick's face and fall onto the counter.

NICK
I'm so sorry. You're right, man. I did all that shit. You're more than I ever was. You're a big man, and I'm a small one. You deserve to live out there. I deserve to live in here.

LITTLE BROTHER
I still believe you can change. After all is said and done, I still love you for being my brother.

NICK
I love you too. What are you doing now? I just want to know what happened with you.

LITTLE BROTHER
I own a pizza chain in Seattle. We're expanding. You always said I couldn't do it. I did it, Nick. I really did it.

Nick smiles through his tears.

NICK
President Olson. That's fucking amazing. I'm so proud of you, man.

A guard taps his watch. Nick presses one hand to the glass.

NICK
Bye.

Little Brother raises his hand to Nick's. Nick is led away without looking back.

INT. MILWAUKEE AIRPORT BOARDING GATE - DAY

Little Brother joins the line for his flight home.

He looks down at his boarding pass. The name on it reads PETER OLSON.

NICK (V.O.)
President Olson.

The scanner chirps.

LITTLE BROTHER
That's me. President Peter Olson.

Peter boards the plane with his head high.

LITTLE BROTHER (V.O.)
This has been Nick and me.

FADE OUT.

>THE END<
